diff --git a/docs/development.md b/docs/development.md
index 73cea7e..3245af7 100644
--- a/docs/development.md
+++ b/docs/development.md
@@ -477,6 +477,8 @@ It skips the iOS shaders, the Xcode project and the IPA. For Mario Kart 8 Deluxe
 cd "<NSP folder>/<id>_build/run" && DM_ROOT="<NSP folder>/<id>_build" DM_ROMFS="<NSP folder>/<id>_build/extract/<version>/RomFS.img" DM_SAVE="<NSP folder>/<id>_build/run/save" DM_TEMP="<NSP folder>/<id>_build/run/temp" DM_MSL_DIR="<NSP folder>/<id>_build/shader/cache" DM_FONT_DIR="<NSP folder>/<id>_build/font" DM_NVN_SHMAP="<repository>/dm/games/<id>/shader_map.tsv" DM_LANG=en-US DM_WINDOW=1 DM_AUDIO=1 "<NSP folder>/<id>_build/tools/dmhost" "<NSP folder>/<id>_build/patched_<version>"
 ```
 
+Resolution upscaling renders an NVN game's 3D at a multiple of its own resolution, so the picture is sharper on a Retina or 4K screen: `DM_UPSCALE=1.5` or `2` (1, the default, is the game's resolution). In a Mac app it is the **Render resolution** setting ([Saved choices](frontends.md#saved-choices): `launcher.DM_UPSCALE`, which the launcher puts into `DM_UPSCALE`); textures keep the size they were made at, so a choice applies at the next launch. A `DM_UPSCALE` from the environment or an `env.txt` wins over the saved choice. Render targets of a screen's shape (aspect 1.4 to 1.9, at least 320x180, single level, COMPRESSIBLE, DISPLAY or depth) are made at their size × the factor; a colour target with neither flag, or with bytes in CPU-visible storage, is held at the game's size until a pass first draws into it ([A render target taken for data](reference/rendering.md#a-render-target-taken-for-data-at-a-render-resolution-above-1x)), and viewports, scissors, copies, clears, occlusion-query counts and `gl_FragCoord` are converted; integer texel fetches and texture size queries in the translated shaders follow each bound texture's own factor (the support buffer's `render_scale` entries, filled per draw); shadow maps (square), lookup tables, multisample targets (with their resolve destinations), mipmapped and shader-written textures stay at the game's size. When the attachments of a pass disagree (a scaled colour beside an unscaled depth), the scaled ones are demoted to the game's size, with their content drawn down, and stay there for the run; the same happens to a copy or resolve that cannot be drawn as a scaling one. The log names the first 24 scaled textures and every 200th after them (`resolution upscaling: ser=… made 3840x2160`), and each demotion the same way. A game that upscales its own 3D in a compute pass (Hyrule Warriors: Age of Imprisonment's FSR-style pass) reads, above 1x, a scene larger than its output with taps computed for its own size, which blurs the picture: the host writes that output itself, the scaled scene filtered down (`DM_UPSCALE_GAME=bypass`, the default; `keep` runs the game's pass; `DM_UPSCALE_GAME_FILTER=box|tent|lanczos` picks the filter; the rule is in `dm/gfx/nvn/nvn_ups.h`, `nvn_ups_bypass_shape` / `nvn_ups_bypass_ok`). A scripted run that must not depend on a saved choice sets `DM_UPSCALE=1`. The section before `tex_desc` in `dm/gfx/nvn/nvn_mtl.m` has the details. `DM_RENDER_SCALE` (below 1, through the game's own code) is a different switch.
+
 The save is in `<id>_build/run/save` and stays there from run to run. `DM_AOC_DIR` is added when the folder has DLC. `DM_NVN_SHMAP` is added for an NVN game; it names the profile's shader map, or the derived or provisional profile's. A Vulkan or GL game's `DM_MSL_DIR` is `<id>_build/shader`. The switches of `--env-file` are added to the command.
 
 Reuse works as for the IPA. Each shader output has its own key, named after the one chain version: `shader/ios/b-<chain>.key` for the iOS libraries and `shader/mac-b-<chain>.key` for the Mac's (`shader/ios/vk.key` and `shader/vk.key` for a Vulkan game, the same with `gl` for a GL game). An NVN build writes only the requested target's key and compiles only its libraries, with one exception: an iOS build of a folder that holds the current chain's Mac libraries (a Mac-only build's, which its run command loads) builds them again too and writes both keys, since a full `dmsh_build.py` run removes every library of the folder before it rebuilds ([An iOS build after a Mac-only build kept no Mac libraries](reference/shaders.md#an-ios-build-after-a-mac-only-build-kept-no-mac-libraries)). The Mac sources and reflection are written and cached for either target, since paired vertex shaders, `c1_full.tsv` and `cb_extent.tsv` need them. A Mac-only build of a folder already packed as an IPA therefore translates again and compiles the Mac libraries. Either key is taken only while the Mac outputs carry a full build's stamp; `c1_full.tsv` or `cb_extent.tsv` that does not name it is made again from them, as for the IPA. Switching targets translates again because `dmsh_build.py`'s per-shader keys include the iOS compile convention; returning to a target reuses its previously compiled MSL from the Metal compile cache (`metallib/` in `shader/cache/cas.db`). Vulkan and GL builds still compile both targets for an IPA. After a successful shader build, the script removes the libraries of other chain versions (`b-<other>` in `shader/cache` and `shader/ios`, and their keys) and logs `removed old shader libraries b-… (N GB)`. A chain change used to leave about 2 GB per update for Breath of the Wild. It then removes the per-shader cache entries (`shader/cache/cas/shader`) the current chain's last full build did not list, and logs `removed old shader cache entries: N (X GB)`; Tears of the Kingdom kept 12 GB of them from six updates ([Old per-shader cache entries](reference/shaders.md#old-per-shader-cache-entries-grew-a-games-build-folder-with-each-chain-change)). The Metal compile cache is not touched.
diff --git a/docs/env_switches.tsv b/docs/env_switches.tsv
