+### The game's own upscaler resolved by the host
+
+- **Symptom:** with resolution upscaling (`DM_UPSCALE=2`) a game whose 3D is drawn below its output size and enlarged by a compute pass of its own looks softer than at 1x, "pixel blurred", though every render target is scaled and the log shows no demotion but the upscaler's own outputs. Hyrule Warriors: Age of Imprisonment's first field: the 3D at a dynamic 1007x566 to 1439x809 of a 1600x900 scene, made 3200x1800, the upscaler's 1920x1080 output demoted (a written image), and the picture blocky.
+- **Cause:** the game's upscaler (an FSR-1 EASU pass, 48 gathers at normalized coordinates) keeps its thread-group counts and writes its image at the game's size, so it runs on the scaled scene with tap offsets its constants computed for the game's sizes: the taps land between texels and span the factor times their radius, a blur of the larger render, while the host already holds more texels than the output needs. Scaling the dispatch instead would mean scaling every coordinate the kernel derives from its invocation ID (the comment before `ups_pick`).
+- **Fix:** the host writes the output itself (`ups_bypass_encode`, `dm/gfx/msl/ups_resolve.metal`): the scene's rectangle filtered down over each output texel's footprint (Lanczos-2 by default; `DM_UPSCALE_GAME_FILTER=box|tent|lanczos`, read at use), bound as the game's kernel binds the two textures, so the later passes (sharpening, the copy to the swap chain) run unchanged. The rule (`nvn_ups.h`, `t_ups.c`): a dispatch shaped like a spatial upscaler, one sampled 2D texture and one written image and nothing else (`nvn_ups_bypass_shape`, once per shader), whose constant buffer holds EASU's constants for the bound scene (`nvn_ups_fsr_rect`: con1 = 1 / texture size and con0 = viewport / output name the rectangle the kernel reads, which a dynamic resolution moves inside the fixed texture: taking the whole texture showed the picture at 72 % of the frame with stale texels around it), and whose output is larger than that rectangle in the game's pixels, of the same shape, and no larger than the rectangle as the host holds it (`nvn_ups_bypass_ok`). Anything else runs the game's kernel: an indirect dispatch, other constants (logged once), integer / depth / compressed / multisample textures, a rectangle whose scaled size falls under the output (1.5x with the resolution lowered). `DM_UPSCALE_GAME=keep` (the launcher's **Upscaler at 1.5× / 2×**) runs the game's kernel to compare; at 1x nothing changes. Cost on an M2 Pro: Lanczos-2 0.8 ms a frame for 2014x1132 → 1920x1080 (its separable weights computed once per texel and kept in registers by unrolled loops: 2.8 ms with a sine pair per read), box 0.2 ms, tent 0.5 ms, about the game's kernel's own cost (the frame time did not move measurably).
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/gfx/tests GAME=<any> check_t_ups_resolve` (a 1200x675 viewport of a 1600x900 sRGB scene at 2x into 1920x1080 with each filter, under validation and on the encoding thread; `keep`, other constants, equal sizes and 1x leave the dispatch alone) and `t_ups` (the shape, the constants, the sizes).
+- **Found on:** [Hyrule Warriors: Age of Imprisonment](../../dm/games/04003F40232DE000/README.md#resolution-upscaling): KTGL's `b0fe425c26f12012`; shots of the first field at 1x, 2x with the game's kernel and 2x with the resolve.
+
+### A render target taken for data at a render resolution above 1x
+
+- **Symptom:** with resolution upscaling (`DM_UPSCALE=2`) the log shows `resolution upscaling: … demoted to the game's size: a pass mixes scaled and unscaled attachments` for the scene's depth and colour, and the 3D stays at the game's size while the HUD is sharp. Pikmin 4: 9 demotions, the scene at 1920x1080; Super Mario Bros. Wonder: 12.
+- **Cause:** `ups_pick` decides at a texture's first resolution, which is often its descriptor's, before any use, and took two kinds of render target for data copied in: a colour texture with neither `COMPRESSIBLE` nor `DISPLAY` (Wonder's scene colour), and a `COMPRESSIBLE` texture whose storage in a CPU-visible pool holds bytes (Unreal's targets in Pikmin 4: the GPU's rendering never reaches guest memory here, so such a pool holds whatever earlier resources left there). Left at 1x beside the scaled depth, the mixed-pass rule demoted the rest of the scene. Scaling them when made would be wrong too: a texture of either kind can be data the game wrote (SD GUNDAM G GENERATION CROSS RAYS sets `COMPRESSIBLE` on every texture), and a scaled texture is not filled from its storage.
+- **Fix:** both are held (`g_ups_held`): made at the game's size with their data, and promoted by the first pass that draws into them, before its vote (`ups_promote`, the reverse of `ups_demote`: a scaled texture under every name, the content drawn up, an sRGB / linear view made again with `tex_replace`). A texture that is only ever read stays as it was. The flag and size part is `nvn_ups_classify` (scale, leave or hold) in `nvn_ups.h`; `ups_pick` logs why a screen-like render target stays at the game's size (first 40).
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/gfx/tests GAME=<any> check_t_ups` (the three verdicts); a run at `DM_UPSCALE=2` logs `made … when first drawn into` for the promoted targets.
+- **Found on:** [Pikmin 4](../../dm/games/0100B7C00933A000/README.md#render-resolution) (Unreal Engine 4.26: 1 demotion left, a 640x360 buffer a shader writes as an image), [Super Mario Bros. Wonder](../../dm/games/010015100B514000/README.md#render-resolution) (none left).
+
